    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "The athletic body image strongly contributed to ED. The athletes self-evaluations about shape, weight and fat percentage and the perceived opinions of others on these aspects appeared to be only significant factors uniquely explaining 14 and 9% of the EDE-Q variance. 64% of the variance was explained by the relevant body image components from daily life and sport."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Limitations of the present study were its sample size and consitution. Unfortunately, the number of Dutch high performance athletes is not that extensive."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future studies should further explore athletes body comparisons in daliy life and sport, and possible differences between athletes with and without disordered eating in this respect. Future research should further explore body images in sport and daily life, particularly in athletes with disordered eating. We needed to extent the sample with a group of at-risk athletes who were not professionaly diagnosed in advance. Future investigation needs to take into account dynamic and contextual body image changes. It should also focus more closely on why certain athletes develop a more negative body image and how this could be prevented."
    },
